1- User opens their browser, enters "www.foobar.com" into their search box
2- Browser asks DNS to switch it to an IP address
3- DNS responds with 8.8.8.8
4- After Browser gets it, it sends HTTP(S) request to that address (on port 80)
5- On that server(machine with IP 8.8.8.8), the web server (Nginx) recieves request firstly
6- Nginx checks: If its a normal static file(files stored as is in the disk, ex: basic HTML files, .png, .jpg), Nginx sends its directly to the browser
7- Nginx checks: If its a non-static file, it sends it to the application server to process and return back the response to Nginx
8- Application server may use database like MySQL to get/store data
9- Application server sends back HTTP(s) response to Nginx
10- Finally, Nginx sends it back to the user browser

What is a server
- Physical/Physical computer that hosts everything with IP 8.8.8.8
What is the role of the domain name
- It's easier than typing numbers.. so its just a name to replace that
What type of DNS record www is in www.foobar.com
- A record which points to a IPv4 address
What is the role of the web server
- Recieves HTTP(s) request, returns static files (if available), and sends non-static files to application server
What is the role of the application server
- Handles Backend code
What is the role of the database
- Stores data
What is the server using to communicate with the computer of the user requesting the website
- It uses HTTP(s) over TCP/IP

Issues with this infrastructure:
SPOF:
- Single Point Of Failure -- Everything is on one server.. server dies -> nothing left
Downtime when maintenance needed (like deploying new code web server needs to be restarted):
- When there's maintenance like updating MySQL database, for example, it will need to restart so the website will go offline for a bit
Cannot scale if too much incoming traffic:
- No Load balancer.. One server can't handle too much traffic..
